### 3.5 Pion Background

The pion spectra are corrected for weak decays (mainly of \(K_{S}^{0}\) and \(\Lambda^{0}\)), muon contribution and background from the detector dead-material interactions. The pion decay muons can be identified as pions due to the similar masses. These background contributions are obtained from PYTHIA 8 SD. Figure 3.17 shows the background contribution to the pion spectra as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) in three ranges of \(\xi\), separately for \(\pi^{-}\) and \(\pi^{+}\). Since there were negligible differences observed between these three ranges of \(\xi\), the background contribution was averaged over \(\xi\). The following parametrization was found to describe it:

\[f_{\rm bkg}^{\pi}\left(p_{\rm T}\right)=a_{0}\exp(a_{1}p_{\rm T})+a_{2}p_{\rm T }^{2}+a_{3}p_{\rm T} \tag{3.7}\]

where \(a_{i}\), \(i=0,\ldots,3\) are free parameters of the fitted function.

The pion background contribution varies between 5% at low-\(p_{\rm T}\) (\(p_{\rm T}=0.25\) GeV/c) and about 1% at \(p_{\rm T}=1.0\) GeV/c for both negatively and positively charged pions. In addition, the background was calculated from EPOS SD+SD\({}^{\prime}\) for the full range of \(\xi\). The differences between PYTHIA 8 and EPOS are up to 1% for \(\pi^{-}\).

### 3.6 Control Plots

Events, in which forward-scattered proton and reconstructed TOF vertex are the result of the same \(pp\) interaction, may originate from ND, DD, SD, and CD processes. It is preferred to estimate the background contribution from data, using dedicated control regions. Since

