Event Corrections and Unfolding Procedure

After subtraction of accidental, DD, CD and ND backgrounds (as described in Sec. 3.5 and 3.6), the data was corrected for detector inefficiencies to obtain the distributions of charged particles and particle to antiparticle (pion, kaon, proton and their antiparticle) multiplicity ratios. These corrections include:

* event-by-event weights due to vertex reconstruction efficiency: \[w_{\rm ev}^{\rm vrt}\left(n_{\rm vrt}^{\rm global},|\Delta z_{0}|\right)=\frac{ 1}{\epsilon_{\rm vrt}\left(n_{\rm vrt}^{\rm global},|\Delta z_{0}|\right)} \cdot\frac{1}{1-f_{\rm vrt}^{\rm veto}\left(n_{\rm vrt}^{\rm global},|\Delta z _{0}|\right)}\] (3.21) where the \(|\Delta z_{0}|\) dependence is only applicable for events with \(n_{\rm vrt}^{\rm global}=2\) as described in Sec. 3.7.
* track-by-track weights due to track reconstruction efficiency, track backgrounds, migrations of tracks into and out of the fiducial region: \[w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta,V_{z}\right)=\frac{1-f_{\rm bkg}\left(p_{\rm T },\eta\right)-f_{\rm fake}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)}{\epsilon_{\rm TPC} \left(p_{\rm T},\eta,V_{z}\right)\epsilon_{\rm TOF}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta,V_{z} \right)}f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\] (3.22) where: \(\epsilon_{\rm TPC}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta,V_{z}\right)\) is TPC track reconstruction efficiency (Sec. 3.7), \(\epsilon_{\rm TOF}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta,V_{z}\right)\) is TOF matching efficiency (Sec. 3.7), \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) is a factor accounting for migrations of tracks into and out of the fiducial region, \(f_{\rm bkg}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) is a fraction of background tracks, and \(f_{\rm fake}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) is a fraction of fake tracks. These corrections were not applied for \(n_{\rm ch}\) measurements since they were taken into account in the unfolding procedure.
* event-by-event (for \(n_{\rm ch}\) distribution ) or track-by-track (for \(p_{\rm T}\), \(\bar{\eta}\) distributions) weights, \(f_{\xi}\), due to migrations of events between three \(\xi\) regions.

Additionally, the obtained distributions were corrected for BBC-small efficiency, \(\epsilon_{\rm BBC}\), using the following weight, which was calculated for each true-level quantity (\(n_{\rm ch}\), \(p_{\rm T}\), \(\bar{\eta}\)) in three ranges of \(\xi\) separately:

\[w_{\rm BBC}=\frac{1}{\epsilon_{\rm BBC}} \tag{3.23}\]

In the following sections, the correction procedure for each of the measured distributions is presented separately.

##### Correction to \(\text{dN}/\text{dn}_{\text{sel}}\)

In order to express the multiplicity distribution in terms of the number of charged particles, \(n_{\rm ch}\), instead of the number of selected tracks, \(n_{\rm sel}\), the following procedure based on the Bayesian unfolding [21, 224] was used. First, the \(n_{\rm sel}\) distribution was corrected for vertex reconstruction effects by applying event-by-event weights, \(w_{\rm ev}^{\rm vrt}(n_{\rm vrt}^{\rm global},|\Delta z_{0}|)\). The number of events in which \(n_{\rm ch}\) are produced, \(N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm ch})\), can be associated with the number of events in which \(n_{\rm sel}\) are reconstructed, \(N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})\). Since there are several possible \(n_{\rm sel}\) observed in \(n_{\rm ch}\) event, \(N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm ch})\) is given by:

\[\begin{array}{rcl}N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm ch})&=&\sum_{n_{\rm sel}=0}P(n_{\rm ch}|n _{\rm sel})\cdot N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})\\ &=&\frac{1}{\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})\epsilon_{r}(n_{\rm ch})}\ \ \sum_{n_{\rm sel}=2}^{8}P(n_{\rm ch}|n _{\rm sel})\cdot N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})\end{array} \tag{3.24}\]

where:

\(P(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel})\) is the conditional probability of having \(n_{\rm ch}\) charged particles in an event in which \(n_{\rm sel}\) tracks were found,

\(\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})\) is a factor, which recovers events that are lost due to TPC track reconstruction and TOF matching inefficiencies, i.e. those with \(n_{\rm ch}\geq 2\) but \(n_{\rm sel}<2\),

\(\epsilon_{r}(n_{\rm ch})\) is a factor, which recovers events which are lost due to fake tracks, i.e. those with \(n_{\rm ch}\leq 8\) but \(n_{\rm sel}>8\). It was checked that this effect is negligible (smaller than \(1\%_{0}\)) and can be omitted.

Figure 3.54 shows \(\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})\) in three ranges of \(\xi\). It was derived from PYTHIA 8 embedding MC and varies from about 25% for \(n_{\rm ch}=2\) to 95% for \(n_{\rm ch}=8\). Since there are additional data-driven corrections to TPC and TOF efficiencies, MC simulations were modified by randomly removing or adding tracks. This was done in accordance with differences in the efficiencies between data and MC. Figure 3.55 shows \(\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})\) calculated in three ranges of \(\xi\) using no-pile-up PYTHIA 8 and EPOS SD+SD\({}^{\prime}\). The differences between these two models, which are up to 8% for \(n_{\rm ch}=2\) and \(0.1<\xi<0.2\), were symmetrized and taken as a systematic uncertainty.

The probability \(P(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel})\) can be derived using Bayes' theorem, which can be stated mathematically in terms of charged particle and charged track multiplicities as:

\[P\left(n_{\rm sel}|n_{\rm ch}\right)\cdot P\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)=P\left(n_{ \rm ch}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot P\left(n_{\rm sel}\right) \tag{3.25}\]

Figure 3.54: \(\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})\) calculated separately in three ranges of \(\xi\) using PYTHIA 8 embedding MC.

where: \(P(n_{\rm sel})\) and \(P(n_{\rm ch})\) are probabilities of observing \(n_{\rm sel}\) and \(n_{\rm ch}\) respectively, \(P(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel})\) and \(P(n_{\rm sel}|n_{\rm ch})\) are conditional probabilities.

In order to improve the estimate of \(P(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel})\), the unfolding is done iteratively:

* In the first iteration, it is assumed that: \[P(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel})=P=P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm sel}|n_{\rm ch})\frac{P^{\rm MC} (n_{\rm ch})}{P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm sel})}\] (3.26) \[N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm ch})=\frac{1}{\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})}\sum_{n_{\rm sel}=2 }^{8}N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})\cdot P\] (3.27) where \(P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm sel}|n_{\rm ch})\), \(P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm ch})\) and \(P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm sel})\) are obtained from MC. \(P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm sel}|n_{\rm ch})\) is the same for each iteration.
* In the \((i+1)\)th iteration we have: \[P^{i+1}=P^{\rm MC}(n_{\rm sel}|n_{\rm ch})\frac{N_{\rm ev}^{i}(n_{\rm ch})}{ N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})}\] (3.28) \[N_{\rm ev}^{i+1}(n_{\rm ch})=\frac{1}{\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})}\sum_{n_{\rm sel }=2}^{8}N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})\cdot P^{i+1}\] (3.29) where \(N_{\rm ev}^{i}(n_{\rm ch})\) is calculated in the previous iteration, and \(N_{\rm ev}(n_{\rm sel})\) is taken from data.

The unfolding matrices \(P(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel})\) for each \(\xi\) region, shown in Fig. 3.56, were obtained from PYTHIA 8 embedding MC and used in all iterations of the above procedure. Similarly to \(\epsilon_{m}(n_{\rm ch})\), the matrices were modified by randomly removing or adding tracks in order to take into account additional data-driven corrections to TPC and TOF efficiencies. In order to increase statistical precision of the unfolding matrices, all simulated events were used,

