The track reconstruction efficiency and the systematic uncertainties related to it were calculated during the analysis of the MB data [21, 22, 232]. The following sources of systematic uncertainties are considered:

#### a) Amount of the Dead-Material

The proper description of the detector dead-material in the simulation is essential to correctly calculate the track reconstruction efficiency. The uncertainty on the amount of dead-material in ATLAS [22, 232, 234] is defined as the sum of the uncertainty on the ID material, which is about \(5\%\), \(10\%\) uncertainty on the IBL material and \(50\%\) uncertainty on the material in the PP0 region. These uncertainties are based on studies [234], in which the rate of secondary vertices (originating from photon conversions and hadronic interactions) and the rate of tracks lost due to interactions in the PP0 region were compared between data and simulation. Figure 4.25 shows the relative systematic uncertainty on track reconstruction efficiency as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(\eta\) due to the above components. Their sum leads to \(1-10\%\) systematic uncertainty due to the description of dead-material in the simulation.

#### b) Probability Cut

Some of low-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged particles are sometimes mismeasured as high-momentum tracks. These tracks mainly originate from particles interacting with the detector dead-material. To suppress fraction of such tracks, the \(\chi^{2}\) fit probability is required to be greater than

Figure 4.24: The track reconstruction efficiency as function of \(p_{\rm T}\) for identified \(\pi^{\pm}\), \(K^{\pm}\), \(\bar{p}\) and \(p\), calculated from PYTHIA 8 A2-MSTW2008LO (SaS).

Figure 4.25: Systematic uncertainty on track reconstruction efficiency as a function of (left) \(\eta\) and (right) \(p_{\rm T}\) due to additional material in the (top) ID, (middle) IBL and (bottom) PP0 region. Figure taken from [232].

0.01 for tracks with \(p_{\rm T}>10\) GeV/c. The systematic uncertainty due to the amount of mismeasured tracks was calculated by comparing the efficiencies of the \(\chi^{2}\) cut between data and simulation. The procedure used Pixel tracklets2 and the topological clusters in the calorimeter system as a reference. The tracks were specified as mismeasured if the ratio of the track \(p_{\rm T}\) to the tracklet (cluster) \(p_{\rm T}\) was significantly larger than 1. The systematic uncertainty on the track reconstruction efficiency was estimated to be about 0.2% and 7% for particles with \(p_{\rm T}\) at 10 and above 50 GeV/c, respectively [232].

Footnote 2: The Pixel tracklet is a track reconstructed only from Pixel hits

#### c) Alignment (High-Momentum Tracks)

A small fraction of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) tracks is affected by some biases due to the detector's misalignments, which can be divided into two categories: deformations that impact the track fit quality and deformations that introduce track parameter biases without changing the track fit quality (so called weak-modes) [232]. The former may be further divided into random misalignment of detector modules and temperature-dependent stave bowing of the IBL, while the latter consists of \(p_{\rm T}\) dependent curvature (or sagitta) bias and smearing. The systematic uncertainties for the weak modes are based on Run-1 data [235, 236] since it was not possible to estimate them with limited amounts of data collected at the beginning of Run-2 [232]. Other systematic uncertainties were assessed by misaligning detector geometry in the simulation by the values obtained from cosmic ray data [237]. The systematic uncertainty related to the above four effects is negligible for tracks with \(p_{\rm T}<10\) GeV/c (majority of tracks used in this analysis), whereas above this value the dominant systematic effect is the random module misalignment (up to 10% for tracks with \(p_{\rm T}=100\) GeV/c).

#### Energy Loss Correction

A pion hypothesis is used during the track reconstruction procedure in order to correct the reconstructed momentum \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm meas}\) for energy loss effects. The correction \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm meas}\) to \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm true}\) due to the rest biases for all particles but the pion, shown in Fig. 4.26, was determined from PYTHIA 8 A2-MSTW2008LO (SaS) MC as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\).

#### Vertex Reconstruction Efficiency

The vertex efficiency, \(\epsilon_{\rm vrt}\), was calculated as a function of the number of selected tracks \(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\_z}\), with all tracks passing the selection criteria described in Sec. 4.2 but the \(z_{0}\) cut. In this analysis, it was required there is a primary vertex built from at least two reconstructed tracks and no secondary vertices with four or more tracks. Therefore, the vertex reconstruction efficiency was determined as the ratio of the number of good reconstructed events (reconstructed primary vertex with \(n_{\rm sel}\geq 2\) and no secondary vertex with four or more tracks) to the number of input events. Figure 4.27 shows the vertex reconstruction efficiency calculated using no-pile-up PYTHIA 8 A2-MSTW2008LO (SaS) sample. Since the charged-particle multiplicity distributions does not depend on additional fake vertices,this MC sample (without pile-up) is sufficient for the studies of vertex reconstruction. The \(\epsilon_{\rm vrt}\) was measured to be \(\approx 0.80\) for events with \(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-}z}=2\), which is about 0.07 lower than for the MB analysis [22], and 1.0 at higher track multiplicities. The efficiency depends on the \(\eta\) and \(p_{\rm T}\) distributions of tracks originating from the vertex, therefore, it is expected that \(\epsilon_{\rm vrt}\) depends on the process under study.

##### Correction to MBTS

The event selection imposed offline signal in at least one MBTS counter on the opposite side of the IP to the ALFA-tagged proton. Such offline MBTS efficiency, \(\epsilon_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}\), was obtained as a function of each measured quantity using PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL).

Figure 4.27: Vertex reconstruction efficiency as a function of \(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-}z}\).

The efficiency was calculated for events within fiducial region as follows:

\[\epsilon_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}=\frac{\mbox{number of MC events satisfying the offline MBTS selection criteria}}{\mbox{number of MC events}} \tag{4.10}\]

Figure 4.28 shows the offline MBTS efficiency, which was obtained from two MC models: PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (default) and EPOS. The \(\epsilon_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}\), obtained from PYTHIA 8 for \(n_{\rm ch}=2\), varies from about 90% for events with \(\xi\) within \(0.035-0.16\) to about 99% for events with \(10^{-5}<\xi<0.035\). The efficiency reaches a plateau of 1 for larger multiplicities, \(n_{\rm ch}>10\), and all ranges of \(\xi\). The efficiency is slightly below 1 for charged particles with \(p_{\rm T}<1.5\) GeV/c and \(\bar{\eta}>-2.5\). Most of the charged particles are produced through the hadronization process, therefore, there is a correlation between their distributions in the central region of rapidity, the value of \(\xi\) and the offline MBTS signal that can be measured. A significant difference is observed between PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO and EPOS predictions. Hence, the differences between these two models were used as a systematic uncertainty. The second systematic uncertainty was calculated using \(\epsilon_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}=1\).

Since the offline MBTS efficiency depends on the charge-particle distributions, it also depends on the process under study. Therefore, the above study was repeated for only SD events and the results are shown in Fig. 4.29.

### 4.6 Migrations into and out of the Fiducial Region

In this section the corrections due to the migrations of tracks and forward-scattered proton into and out of the fiducial region are described.

#### Migrations of Tracks

The fraction of reconstructed tracks \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\), which migrate into the fiducial region (\(p_{\rm T}>0.1\) GeV/c and \(|\eta|<2.5\)) and are associated to primary particles outside the kinematic range (\(p_{\rm T}<0.1\) GeV/c or \(|\eta|>2.5\)), was estimated from MC in MB analysis [22]. The main residual migrations were observed at \(|\eta|\approx 2.5\), where about 11% of reconstructed tracks were associated to a primary particle outside the fiducial region.

There are four contributions, which are added in quadrature and considered as a total systematic uncertainty on the track migration correction:

1. Difference between the \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) corrections estimated from HERWIG and PYTHIA 8 Monash samples, which results in the systematic uncertainty of about 19% in \(p_{\rm T}\) and 12% in \(\eta\).
2. Difference between the \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) corrections estimated from PYTHIA 8 ND samples with nominal geometry and with additional 5% ID material. This results in the systematic uncertainty of about 1% in \(p_{\rm T}\) and 4% in \(\eta\).

Figure 4.28: Offline MBTS efficiency calculated from (left) PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO and (right) EPOS MC models, as a function of (top) \(n_{\rm ch}\), (middle) \(p_{\rm T}\) and (bottom) \(\bar{\eta}\) in three ranges of \(\xi\).

Figure 4.29: Offline MBTS efficiency calculated from (left) PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO SD and (right) EPOS SD+SD\({}^{\prime}\) MC models, as a function of (top) \(n_{\rm ch}\), (middle) \(p_{\rm T}\) and (bottom) \(\bar{\eta}\) in three ranges of \(\xi\).

3. Difference between the \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) corrections estimated from PYTHIA 8 ND samples with nominal geometry and with additional 10% IBL material. This results in the systematic uncertainty of about 4% in \(p_{\rm T}\) and 4% in \(\eta\).
4. Difference between the \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) corrections estimated from PYTHIA 8 ND samples with nominal geometry and with additional \(2\times 10\%\) ID material in the high \(\eta\) region (\(|\eta|>2.2\)). This results in the largest contribution to the total systematic uncertainty and equals to about 76% in \(p_{\rm T}\) and 80% in \(\eta\).

Both, the \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) correction and the total systematic uncertainty on the track migration corrections, are shown in Fig. 4.30.

The correction from the MB analysis (obtained from ND samples) was used. This is not strictly valid since it depends on the process under study and was calculated as a function of \(\eta\) instead of \(\bar{\eta}\). This results in the non-closure of the corrected MC distributions for SD sample, which is observed at \(|\bar{\eta}|\approx 2.5\) (Sec. 4.7). Therefore, any deviations from the closure tests were taken as a systematic uncertainty.

#### Migrations in \(\xi\)

The analysis was performed in three ranges of \(\xi\). Thus, there are migrations into and out of these \(\xi\) regions. They mainly originate from the resolution of \(\xi\), which is measured with the ALFA detectors. The corrections due to migrations into and out of \(\xi\) regions was defined as:

\[f_{\xi}=\frac{1-f_{\xi}^{-}}{1-f_{\xi}^{+}} \tag{4.11}\]

where \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) is the fraction of events for which the corresponding true-level \(\xi_{\rm true}\), is outside of the \(\xi\) region, and \(f_{\xi}^{+}\) is the fraction of events for which the corresponding reconstructed \(\xi_{\rm reco}\) is outside of the \(\xi\) region.

Figure 4.30: (left) Fraction of selected tracks migrating from outside of the kinematic range to the signal region as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(\eta\), and (right) the total systematic uncertainty on the \(f_{\rm okr}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) correction.

The \(f_{\xi}\) was calculated for each measured variable separately. Figures 4.31 to 4.33 show the fraction of events \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) and \(f_{\xi}^{+}\) as a function of \(n_{\rm ch}\), \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(\bar{\eta}\), calculated using PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO inelastic sample. The largest migrations into and out of the \(\xi\) regions were observed at \(0.035<\xi<0.08\), and at \(10^{-5}<\xi<0.035\), \(n_{\rm ch}>40\), where they are of the order of \(6-8\%\). In the other \(\xi\) regions, \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) and \(f_{\xi}^{+}\) are smaller than \(2\%\). Moreover, the differences between \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) and \(f_{\xi}^{+}\) are smaller than \(2\%\) in almost all bins. This study was repeated for the analysis of SD events (non-SD background subtracted distributions).

In order to calculate systematic uncertainties, the shape of \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) and \(f_{\xi}^{+}\), normalized to unity, was taken from data, whereas the total normalization of migrations into and out of the fiducial region was calculated using MC. Corrections were defined as:

\[f_{\xi}=\frac{1-f_{\xi}^{-,\;\rm total}\cdot f_{\xi}^{-,\;\rm data}}{1-f_{\xi} ^{+,\;\rm total}\cdot f_{\xi}^{+,\;\rm data}} \tag{4.12}\]

where \(f_{\xi}^{-,\;\rm total}\) is the total fraction of events in the MC for which the corresponding \(\xi_{\rm true}\), is outside of the \(\xi\) region, \(f_{\xi}^{+,\;\rm total}\) is the total fraction of events in the MC for which the corresponding \(\xi_{\rm reco}\) is outside of the \(\xi\) region, \(f_{\xi}^{-,\;\rm data}\) and \(f_{\xi}^{+,\;\rm data}\) are the shapes of \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) and \(f_{\xi}^{+}\), taken from the corresponding \(\xi\) bins in the data. In particular, \(f_{\xi}^{+,\;\rm data}\) has the same shape as signal.

