Figure 4.32: (red) Fraction of events \(f_{\xi}^{-}\) and (blue) \(f_{\xi}^{+}\) as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) in three ranges of \(\xi\). The values of \(f_{\xi}\) are shown in the bottom panels.

Event Corrections and Unfolding Procedure

In this section, the corrections applied to the measured distributions are described. First, the accidental, and non-SD (if applicable) backgrounds were subtracted from the uncorrected distributions, i.e. the inclusive distributions of charged particles and particle to antiparticle (pion, kaon, proton and their antiparticle) multiplicity ratios. Next, the data was corrected for detector inefficiencies by applying:

* event-by-event weights reflecting trigger and vertex reconstruction efficiencies: \[w_{\mathrm{ev}}\left(n_{\mathrm{sel}}^{\mathrm{no-z}},n_{\mathrm{MBTS}}\right)= \frac{1}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{vrt}}\left(n_{\mathrm{sel}}^{\mathrm{no-z}}\right)} \cdot\frac{1}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{trg}}\left(n_{\mathrm{MBTS}}\right)}\] (4.13) where \(\epsilon_{\mathrm{trg}}\left(n_{\mathrm{MBTS}}\right)\) and \(\epsilon_{\mathrm{vrt}}\left(n_{\mathrm{sel}}^{\mathrm{no-z}}\right)\) are the trigger and the vertex reconstruction efficiencies, respectively.
* track-by-track weights related to the track reconstruction efficiency, track backgrounds from non-primary tracks and migrations of tracks into the fiducial region: \[w_{\mathrm{trk}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)=\frac{1-f_{\mathrm{bkg}} \left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)-f_{\mathrm{fake}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta \right)-f_{\mathrm{okr}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{ trk}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)}\] (4.14) where \(\epsilon_{\mathrm{trk}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)\) is the track reconstruction efficiency, \(f_{\mathrm{bkg}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)\), \(f_{\mathrm{fake}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)\) and \(f_{\mathrm{okr}}\left(p_{\mathrm{T}},\eta\right)\) are the fractions of background, fake and outside of the kinematical range tracks, respectively.
* event-by-event (for \(n_{\mathrm{ch}}\) distribution) or track-by-track (for \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) and \(\bar{\eta}\) distributions) weights, \(f_{\xi}\), due to migrations of events between three \(\xi\) regions.

Additionally, the obtained distributions were corrected for offline MBTS efficiency, \(\epsilon_{\mathrm{MBTS}}^{\mathrm{offline}}\), using the following weight:

\[w_{\mathrm{MBTS}}^{\mathrm{offline}}=\frac{1}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{MBTS}}^{ \mathrm{offline}}} \tag{4.15}\]

In the following sections the correction procedure for each of the measured distributions is presented separately. The uncorrected distributions in a wider range are shown in Fig. 4.16.

Apart from the measurements of charged particle distributions, the shapes of \(\xi\) and \(|t|\) distributions were also obtained. The results were only corrected for MBTS trigger efficiency and ALFA acceptance (described in Sec. 4.2). Since this measurement was not considered as the main aim of this thesis, no systematics was obtained for these distributions.

#### Correction to \(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{d}\mathrm{n}_{\mathrm{sel}}\)

After the subtraction of accidental and non-SD backgrounds (if applicable), and applying the \(w_{\mathrm{ev}}\left(n_{\mathrm{sel}}^{\mathrm{no-z}},n_{\mathrm{MBTS}}\right)\) weights to the distribution of \(n_{\mathrm{sel}}\), the procedure based on the Bayesian unfolding was used to express the multiplicity distribution in terms of the number of charged particles \(n_{\rm ch}\). Changes, in comparison to the STAR analysis (Sec. 3.9), are only needed to the \(\epsilon_{r}\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\) and \(\epsilon_{m}\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\) factors. The former was not necessary, since in this analysis there was no upper limit on \(n_{\rm ch}\) in the fiducial region definition.

The applied correction for events, which are lost due to tracking inefficiencies, i.e. \(n_{\rm sel}<2\), but have \(n_{\rm ch}\geq 2\), is given as follows [17]:

\[\epsilon_{m}\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)=1-\left(1-\epsilon_{\rm trk}^{m}\right)^{n _{\rm ch}}-n_{\rm ch}\cdot\epsilon_{\rm trk}^{m}\cdot\left(1-\epsilon_{\rm trk }^{m}\right)^{n_{\rm ch}-1} \tag{4.16}\]

where the \(\epsilon_{\rm trk}^{m}\) is the mean track reconstruction efficiency in the first \(n_{\rm sel}\) bin and is equal to about 0.71 in ATLAS.

The unfolding matrices \(P\left(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\) for each \(\xi\) region, which were obtained from PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL) and used in the first iteration of the unfolding procedure, are shown Fig. 4.34. The unfolding matrices for the analysis of only SD events were calculated separately.

Figure 4.34: The unfolding matrices filled using PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL) for three ranges of \(\xi\) separately.

The \(dN/dn_{\rm ch}\) distribution, obtained after the unfolding procedure, was corrected for off-line MBTS efficiency, through \(w_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}\) weights, and migrations of events between \(\xi\) ranges, through \(f_{\xi}\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\) weights. Since the unfolding matrices contain track reconstruction efficiencies, track backgrounds and migrations of tracks into the fiducial region, the weight \(w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) was not used. The total number of events, \(N_{\rm ev}=N\), was calculated as the integral of the unfolded \(n_{\rm ch}\) distribution.

#### Correction to Pseudorapidity Distribution

First, the accidental and non-SD backgrounds (if applicable) were subtracted. Next, the tracks were corrected for detector effects by applying the \(w_{\rm ev}\left(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-}z},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\), \(w_{\rm trk}\left(\eta,p_{\rm T}\right)\), \(w_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}\) and \(f_{\xi}\left(\bar{\eta}\right)\) weights. The unfolding procedure was unnecessary in this case because the \(\eta\) resolution is significantly smaller than bin widths. The corrected \(\bar{\eta}\) distributions were normalized by the number of events \(N_{ev}\) in order to obtain the charged particle multiplicity distributions. In addition, the distributions were normalized to unity, hence, it was possible to compare only their shapes with the MC predictions. Moreover, the mean \(\bar{\eta}\) in an event, \(\langle\bar{\eta}\rangle\), was obtained from the measured distributions.

#### Correction to Transverse Momentum Distribution

After the subtraction of accidental and non-SD (if applicable) backgrounds, the tracks were corrected for detector effects by applying the \(w_{\rm ev}\left(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-}z},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\) and \(w_{\rm trk}\left(\eta,p_{\rm T}\right)\) weights. Then, the obtained distributions were used as an input to the Bayesian unfolding procedure, which was similar to the one used for the \(n_{\rm ch}\) distribution. The unfolding matrices contain the relationship between the reconstructed track \(p_{\rm T}\) and the \(p_{\rm T}\) of primary particles. By this, the resolution and migration effects were included in the correction procedure. The distributions, obtained after the unfolding procedure, were further corrected for off-line MBTS efficiency, through \(w_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}\) weights, and migrations of events between \(\xi\) ranges, through \(f_{\xi}\left(p_{\rm T}\right)\) weights. Finally, the distributions were normalized either to the number of events, \(N_{\rm ev}\), or to the number of particles. In addition, the mean transverse momentum in an event, \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\), was obtained from the measured distributions.

Figure 4.35 shows the \(p_{\rm T}\) unfolding matrices for three ranges of \(\xi\), which were obtained from PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL). The unfolding matrices for the analysis of only SD events were calculated separately.

#### Correction to Mean Transverse Momentum Distribution

The \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) vs. \(n_{\rm ch}\) was defined by \(\left(\sum_{i}p_{\rm T}\left(i\right)\right)/\left(\sum_{i}1\right)\) as a function of \(n_{\rm ch}\)[21, 22], where \(i\) runs over all particles within a given \(n_{\rm ch}\) category. In the correction procedure, first the \(\sum_{i}p_{\rm T}\left(i\right)\) and \(\sum_{i}1\) as a function of \(n_{\rm sel}\) were corrected for the accidental and non-SD (if applicable) backgrounds. Then, the \(w_{\rm ev}\left(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-}z},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\) and \(w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\) weights were applied to correct the number of selected tracks in the \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(n_{\rm sel}\) bin to the number of chargedparticles in that bin. As a result the following distributions were obtained:

\[N^{n_{\rm ch}}\left(n_{\rm sel}\right) =N_{\rm ev}\int dp_{\rm T}\cdot P\left(n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot \rho_{\rm sel}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot w_{ev}\left(n_{\rm sel}^ {\rm no\mbox{-}z},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\cdot w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta \right)\cdot p_{\rm T} \tag{4.17}\] \[D^{n_{\rm ch}}\left(n_{\rm sel}\right) =N_{\rm ev}\int dp_{\rm T}\cdot P\left(n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot \rho_{\rm sel}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot w_{\rm ev}\left(n_{\rm sel }^{\rm no\mbox{-}z},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\cdot w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right) \tag{4.18}\]

where \(N^{n_{\rm ch}}\left(n_{\rm sel}\right)\) and \(D^{n_{\rm ch}}\left(n_{\rm sel}\right)\) are corrected distributions of \(\sum_{i}p_{\rm T}\left(i\right)\) and \(\sum_{i}1\) as a function of \(n_{\rm sel}\), \(\rho_{\rm sel}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\) is the density of tracks (defined as the number of tracks per event per unit of \(p_{\rm T}\) in a given \(n_{\rm sel}\) bin), \(P(n_{\rm sel})\) is the probability of observing \(n_{\rm sel}\) tracks.

Using the unfolding matrices, determined in the final unfolding iteration of the

Figure 4.35: The unfolding matrices used for \(p_{\rm T}\) distribution, filled using PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL) for three ranges of \(\xi\) separately.

distributions (Sec. 4.7), the two above distributions were expressed as a function of \(n_{\rm ch}\):

\[N\left(n_{\rm ch}\right) =\int dp_{\rm T}\cdot\rho_{\rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm ch}\right) \cdot p_{\rm T} \tag{4.19}\] \[D\left(n_{\rm ch}\right) =\int dp_{\rm T}\cdot\rho_{\rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm ch}\right)\] (4.20) \[\rho_{\rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm ch}\right) =\sum_{n_{\rm sel}=2}P\left(n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot P\left(n_{\rm ch}|n_{ \rm sel}\right)\cdot\rho_{\rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right) \tag{4.21}\]

where \(\rho_{\rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\) is the number of charged particles per event per unit of \(p_{\rm T}\) in a given \(n_{\rm sel}\) bin, \(P\left(n_{\rm ch}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\) is the probability of \(n_{\rm ch}\) under condition of \(n_{\rm sel}\).

The distributions, obtained after the unfolding procedure, \(N\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\) and \(D\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\), were corrected for offline MBTS efficiency, through \(w_{\rm MBTS}^{\rm offline}\) weights, and migrations of events between \(\xi\) ranges, through \(f_{\xi}\) weights.

Finally, the \(\left\langle p_{\rm T}\right\rangle\) as a function of \(n_{\rm ch}\) was calculated as a ratio of \(N\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\) and \(D\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)\):

\[\left\langle p_{\rm T}\right\rangle\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)=\frac{N\left(n_{ \rm ch}\right)}{D\left(n_{\rm ch}\right)}=\frac{\int dp_{\rm T}\cdot\rho_{\rm ch }\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm ch}\right)\cdot p_{\rm T}}{\int dp_{\rm T}\cdot\rho_{ \rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm ch}\right)} \tag{4.22}\]

In the above procedure there were two assumptions:

1. The correction weights, \(w_{\rm ev}\left(n_{\rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-z}},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\) and \(w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T},\eta\right)\), were used to transform the number of selected tracks in a given \(\left(n_{\rm sel},p_{\rm T}\right)\) bin to the number of particles in the same bin: \[\rho_{\rm ch}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right) =\rho_{\rm sel}\left(p_{\rm T}|n_{\rm sel}\right)\cdot w_{\rm ev}\left(n_{ \rm sel}^{\rm no\mbox{-z}},n_{\rm MBTS}\right)\cdot w_{\rm trk}\left(p_{\rm T },\eta\right)\] (4.23) However, this procedure may not be valid because track reconstruction efficiency may slightly depend on the \(n_{\rm sel}\). The three-dimensional track reconstruction efficiencies, calculated as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\), \(\eta\) and \(n_{\rm sel}\), would be needed to check this assumption.
2. The \(p_{\rm T}\) of the tracks was not expected to be changed when migrating from a given \(n_{\rm sel}\) bin to the corresponding \(n_{\rm ch}\) bin. The full two-dimensional unfolding of \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(n_{\rm ch}\) would be needed to check this assumption.

However, any deviations from the above assumptions were covered by the non-closure of the corrected MC distributions and were taken as a systematic uncertainty.

#### Closure Tests

In order to validate the correction procedures, closure tests were performed, i.e. full correction procedure was applied to the MC detector-level distributions and the results were directly compared to the true-level distributions. Figure 4.36 shows closure tests of multiplicity, transverse momentum, pseudorapidity and \(\left\langle p_{\rm T}\right\rangle\)(\(n_{\rm ch}\)) distributions for three ranges of \(\xi\), separately. PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL) was used as an input. In order to compare corrected and true-level distributions, the statistical uncertainties of the true-level distributions were assumed to be 0. The difference between true-level and corrected distributions was taken as a systematic uncertainties.

#### Particle Identification

In this section the particle identification via the \(dE/dx\) measured by the Pixel detector (including IBL) is described. The specific energy loss \(dE/dx\) is a function of the particle momentum magnitude. The \(dE/dx\) distribution within given \(p_{\rm T}\) bin is additionally smeared because of the momentum variation with rapidity. Therefore, the analysis of identified particles was limited to a very narrow pseudorapidity region of \(|\eta|<0.5\), in which different particle species are well separated. This is important for this analysis due to generally poor \(dE/dx\) resolution of the ATLAS detector.

The procedure used for the particle identification follows the steps described in [209, 210]. The Bethe-Bloch inspired parametric function describing the MPV of the \(dE/dx\) as a function of \(\beta\gamma\) is given by:

\[{\rm MPV}_{dE/dx}\left(\beta\gamma\right)=\frac{p_{1}}{\left(f\left(\beta \gamma\right)\right)^{p_{3}}}\ln\left(1+(p_{2}\beta\gamma)^{p_{5}}\right)-p_{4} \tag{4.24}\]

Figure 4.36: Closure tests of (top left) multiplicity, (top right) mean transverse momentum as a function of multiplicity, (bottom left) transverse momentum and (bottom right) pseudorapidity distributions for three ranges of \(\xi\) using PYTHIA 8 A3-NNPDF20LO (DL). The ratios of corrected to true-level distributions are shown in the bottom panels.

\[f\left(\beta\gamma\right)=\frac{\left(\beta\gamma\right)^{2}}{1+\left(\beta\gamma \right)^{2}} \tag{4.25}\]

where \(\beta\gamma=p/m\), \(p\) is the momentum magnitude of the measured particle, i.e. pion, kaon or proton, \(m\) is its mass, \(p_{1}-p_{5}\) are free parameters that does not rely on any prior knowledge of the particle species.

The data sample of reconstructed tracks with \(0.3<p_{\mathrm{T}}<1.0\) GeV/c was divided into 10 transverse momentum slices, which are equal in \(\ln\left(p_{\mathrm{T}}\right)\). To extract the particle yield for a given particle type, the \(dE/dx\) distribution for each of the three input mass hypotheses (\(\pi\), \(K\), \(p\)) in each of the above transverse momentum slices was described by the ExpGausExp function [238] (consisting of the Gaussian core and exponential tails on both sides of the Gaussian). In the other ATLAS analyses [209, 210], a Crystal Ball function [239, 240] was used to model the \(dE/dx\) distribution in each \(\ln\left(p_{\mathrm{T}}\right)\) bin. However, it was found during this analysis that better fit quality and stability is achieved with the ExpGausExp function. The ExpGausExp function is defined as:

\[f\left(m;k_{L},k_{H}\right)=\begin{cases}\exp\left[\frac{k_{L}^{2}}{2}+k_{L}m \right],&\text{ for }m\leq-k_{L}\\ \exp\left[-\frac{1}{2}m^{2}\right],&\text{ for }-k_{L}<m\leq k_{H}\\ \exp\left[\frac{k_{H}^{2}}{2}-k_{H}m\right],&\text{ for }k_{H}<m\end{cases} \tag{4.26}\]

\[m\left(\ln\left(dE/dx\right);\text{MPV}_{dE/dx}\left(\beta\gamma\right), \sigma\right)=\left(\ln\left(dE/dx\right)-\ln\left(\text{MPV}_{dE/dx}\left( \beta\gamma\right)\right)\right)/\sigma \tag{4.27}\]

where \(\sigma\) is the standard deviation of the Gaussian core, \(k_{L}\) and \(k_{H}\) are the decay constants of the exponentials on the low and high side tails.

All of the logarithmic \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) slices were fitted simultaneously with a sum of three ExpGausExp functions. The MPV of the \(dE/dx\) had to follow the parametrization given by Eq. (4.24) with the transverse momentum corrected for the energy loss effects (as described in Sec. 4.5). The relative fraction \(n_{i}^{j}\) of each particle, \(i=\pi\), \(K\), \(p\), in each interval \(j\) of \(\ln\left(p_{\mathrm{T}}\right)\), was left free in the fit. Hence there were \(5+3+2\cdot 10\) free parameters in the fit, i.e. \(p_{1}-p_{5}\), \(\sigma\), \(k_{L}\), \(k_{H}\) and \(n_{\pi}^{j}\), \(n_{K}^{j}\) in \(10\,\ln\left(p_{\mathrm{T}}\right)\) slices (the fraction of (anti)protons was defined as \(n_{p}^{j}=1-n_{\pi}^{j}-n_{K}^{j}\)). The results depend slightly on the charge of analysed tracks and the number of hits used for \(dE/dx\) calculation (so-called good \(dE/dx\) hits). Hence, the fit was done separately for 6 cases: negative and positive tracks, having 2, 3 or more than 3 good \(dE/dx\) hits used for \(dE/dx\) calculation.

Figure 4.37 shows the distribution of \(dE/dx\) as a function of the rigidity \(q\times p\), together with the fitted MPV of \(dE/dx\) for each particle species, for tracks having 3 good \(dE/dx\) hits (most of tracks has 3 hits used for the \(dE/dx\) calculation, what is shown in the distribution presented in the same figure).

Figures 4.38 and 4.39 show sample \(dE/dx\) fit (positive tracks with three good \(dE/dx\) hits) in a few \(\ln\left(p_{\mathrm{T}}\right)\) slices, as well as the \(\text{MPV}_{dE/dx}\left(\beta\gamma\right)\) for pions, kaons and protons.

### Antiparticle-to-Particle Ratios

The following steps were performed to correct an identified antiparticle to particle (pion, kaon, proton and their antiparticle) multiplicity ratios as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) (the ratios were averaged over three ranges of \(\xi\) due to the limited statistics):

* The raw particle yields were obtained through fits to the \(dE/dx\) distributions (described in Sec. 4.7), where the trigger and vertex reconstruction efficiencies were applied.
* The \(dE/dx\) fits were performed separately for tracks having 2, 3 or more than 3 good \(dE/dx\) hits. The results from these three fits were added up in this step.
* The \(p_{\rm T}\) distributions for each particle species were shifted by the size of energy loss correction.
* The accidental backgrounds were not subtracted. It was assumed that they do not depend on the particle type.
* The particle \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra were corrected for track reconstruction efficiencies. Also, the backgrounds from non-primary tracks were subtracted. Since fake track contribution is the same for particle and antiparticle, it was not subtracted. The ratio of particle to antiparticle corrections is shown in Fig. 4.40.
* Each antiparticle \(p_{\rm T}\) distribution was divided by the corresponding particle \(p_{\rm T}\) distribution in order to obtain fully corrected identified antiparticle to particle multiplicity ratios.

